<section id="section-2-1-upping-the-dx" class="flex flex-col mt-(--spacing-lg) gap-y-(--spacing-sm) scroll-mt-(--spacing-md)"><h2 class="text-(--custom-color-header) scroll-mt-(--spacing-md) [--inline-code-border:] [--inline-code-px:var(--spacing-xs)] [--inline-code-aside-ps:none] [--inline-code-mx:0.1em] break-words font-bold text-4xl mb-sm">Upping the DX</h2><p class=""><figure class="w-full overflow-hidden mx-auto py-sm flex flex-col items-center gap-y-default"><img class="max-h-[375px] w-full rounded-lg object-contain" src="/validation.jpg" alt="SpongeBob saying Validation overlaid over rainbow"/><figcaption class="max-w-[60%] font-normal italic text-center text-xs text-(--custom-color-dimmest)">SpongeBob saying Validation overlaid over rainbow</figcaption></figure></p><p class="">The manual validator we wrote in Day 3 was fine for one-off examples but the process quickly becomes tedious and error-prone. Therefore to improve our Developer Experience (DX) and consequently ensure code robustness we will use a data validation library (<em class="inline-block mx-1">Zod</em>) that streamlines the process for us.</p></section>
